# Which machines the UNATTENDED hourly sweep may arm a purge on.
#
# The owner's ruling: per-machine interactive arming meant a headless home machine could never
# execute a purge and a fleet-wide purge would stay permanently half-applied there, so
# unattended arming is declared per machine here.
#
# 🔴 WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT REMOVE FROM THE PATH. A purge already requires all of:
#
#   1. a proposed plan naming the checkout and the HOST/OWNER/REPO coordinate,
#   2. an explicit approval on the originating machine, with the whole fleet-wide consequence
#      printed and an `Are you sure?` — including `THERE IS NO UNDO`,
#   3. arming on the machine that executes.
#
# Naming a machine here removes **only step 3, and only on that machine**. Steps 1 and 2 are
# untouched, so this does not create an unreviewed deletion: it makes an approval you have
# already given take effect without a second visit. That is the whole distinction, and it is
# why this file is a committed declaration rather than a setting — it is reviewable in a diff,
# revocable in a diff, and every machine can see what every other machine is permitted to do.
#
# One selector per line: a machine key from `machines.md`, a label, or `all`. Lines are joined
# as any-of, so rolling out one box at a time is the natural use. `#` comments and blanks are
# ignored.
#
# 🔴 AN EMPTY OR MISSING FILE ARMS NOBODY, AND THAT IS NOT WHAT THE SELECTOR GRAMMAR DOES ON
# ITS OWN. `machine.selector_matches` treats an EMPTY spec as matching EVERY machine — which is
# the right default for "which machines does this skill apply to" and catastrophic here, since a
# truncated or accidentally-emptied file would arm the entire fleet for an irreversible act.
# `purge.sweep_may_arm` therefore refuses an empty selector BEFORE consulting the grammar, and
# `tests/test_purge_arming.py` pins that in both directions.
#
# To revoke, delete the line and commit. Until the sweep picks it up (hourly) the machine stays
# armed, so for an urgent revocation also run `bin/sync.py` on it, or neutralise it there by
# removing the approval — which is faster, being machine-reachable without this file.

# Lukas, 2026-10-01: *"I want it to work. Make it work."*
#
# `all` rather than a list, and the reasoning is the point: ANY machine left off this list
# reproduces exactly the defect he rejected, just smaller. A purge that completes on two of
# three machines is still reported done while material stays on the third — the
# permanently-half-applied state the whole repo-lifecycle loop exists to end. A partial
# declaration is that bug with a shorter blast radius, not a safer design.
#
# What protects him is steps 1 and 2 above, which this does not touch. Narrowing this to
# specific machine keys is a one-line diff if he wants it.
all
